; Format 3: the language topics. What a language calls the people, places, events, stories and sections of the common
; topics (xtopics), taken from its own aligned text and outlines, and the topics only it has. A core, and one part
; per book with the verses each name came from.

topics-segment = body<topics-header, topics-tables>
topics-tables = topics-core-tables / topics-part-tables

topics-header = {
  "class": "topics",
  "v": 1,
  "tables": { * table-name => [uint, bool] },
  "lang": tstr,
  "part": "core" / book-code,
  ? "book": book-code,         ; absent in the core; in a part, absent or equal to "part"
  "res": [* [tstr, tstr]],     ; every source in the core; required and empty in a part
}

topics-core-tables = {
  "names": { * topic-id => [+ topic-name] },   ; required, v1; most frequent first (count descending),
                               ; ties by name in code point order; a name and source pair is listed once
  ? "local": local-topics,     ; optional, v1; "{lang}:tema/..." topics only this language has
  "coverage": coverage,        ; required, v1
}

; "source" says where the name came from: the aligned text ("alignment"), the language's own book outline
; ("outline"), its OBS ("obs") or a list of names the builder was given ("list"). "count" is how many times the name
; was found; "books" the books it was found in.
;
; How an "alignment" name is taken (normative). For each TIPNR name line of a person or place (its dStrong and its
; references; a reference with an occurrence letter a or b means the first or second occurrence of that original word
; in the verse, and with no letter every occurrence), the gateway words aligned to that original word in that verse
; are found, and only in the verses TIPNR lists for that topic. The name is the maximal run of consecutive word pieces
; (only " " between them) that contains the first word aligned to that original word; other words aligned to it in
; that verse are not part of the name and are counted in the build report. A reference TIPNR writes in English
; versification is converted to the original's numbering first; one that cannot be converted is listed in the report.
; Names are stored in NFC, as the builder normalized them before storing; they are not lowercased, and a reader never
; normalizes (container.md): it shows and compares the bytes it reads. A topic of kind event takes no name from the
; alignment (ids.md).
topic-name = [name: tstr, source: "alignment" / "outline" / "obs" / "list", count: uint, books: book-set]

topics-part-tables = {
  ? "names": verse-lists,      ; optional, v1; "keys" are topic ids, sorted by code point; per key, the verses of this
                               ; book from which the topic's alignment names were taken. Which name came from which
                               ; verse is not stored: the core has the counts
}
